    title: "The Persian Empire and classical Greece",
    hegemon: "<strong>Persia (the Achaemenids)</strong> is the first true world empire – from Egypt to India, ~40% of the world's population. It rules with striking tolerance: local religions and laws remain, royal roads and uniform coinage connect everything. It is checked first by the Greeks (490/480 BC), then undone by a single man: Alexander of Macedon conquers it completely in 334–330 BC.",
    text: "<p>Cyrus the Great creates the largest empire yet seen in only 30 years. The small, quarrelling Greek city-states repel the Persian invasions (Marathon 490, Salamis 480 BC) – a turning point: Athens rises to naval and cultural supremacy.</p><p>Athens' “Golden Age” follows: democracy under Pericles, the Parthenon, tragedy, historiography, philosophy. The fratricidal war between Athens and Sparta (Peloponnesian War, 431–404 BC) ruins both – and clears the way for Macedon.</p>",
