    hegemon: "<strong>Rome</strong> formally remains hegemon but staggers: in the 3rd century more than 20 soldier-emperors rule within 50 years. Diocletian and Constantine save the empire by division and reorganization – but in 395 it splits for good into East and West. Wealthy <strong>Eastern Rome (Byzantium)</strong> weathers the storms; the West is taken over by Germanic peoples – in 476 Odoacer deposes the last emperor.",
    text: "<p>Why did Rome fall? Not one cause but many: permanent war on two fronts (Germanic tribes, Persia), inflation, plagues, civil wars, crushing taxation – and finally the Huns, who trigger the Migration Period from 375. Goths, Vandals and Franks push into the empire; in 410 Alaric sacks Rome itself.</p><p>In parallel, the quiet revolution: Constantine permits Christianity in 313; in 380 it becomes the state religion. The Church adopts the empire's structures – and outlives it. In China the Han dynasty collapses at the same time (220); centuries of division follow there too.</p>",
