    title: "The World Wars – Europe's self-destruction",
    hegemon: "Europe destroys its own supremacy. After 1918 <strong>Great Britain</strong> leads only in appearance; in reality the <strong>USA</strong> has long been the strongest economic and financial power – but withdraws into isolationism. This power vacuum, the Great Depression and the losers' thirst for revenge enable the dictators' rise. In 1945 Europe lies in ruins; the USA and the Soviet Union remain as superpowers.",
    text: "<p>The <b>First World War</b> (1914–18) becomes the ur-catastrophe: battles of matériel (Verdun, the Somme – ~1 million casualties each for kilometres of ground), poison gas, ~17 million dead. Four empires fall (Germany, Austria-Hungary, Russia, the Ottomans). In Russia, Lenin's Bolsheviks seize power in 1917 – communism becomes a state. The Treaty of Versailles humiliates Germany without permanently weakening it – “an armistice for twenty years” (Foch).</p><p>The <b>Great Depression</b> from 1929 (Black Thursday) plunges the world into misery and radicalizes politics: in 1933 <b>Hitler</b> becomes chancellor, builds a dictatorship within months and steers toward war. The <b>Second World War</b> (1939–45) becomes history's bloodiest conflict with ~60–70 million dead. In the <b>Holocaust</b> the National Socialists murder six million Jews – the rupture of civilization itself. Turning points: Stalingrad (1942/43), D-Day (1944); in August 1945 two atomic bombs force Japan's surrender – humanity can now destroy itself.</p>",
    denken: "Faith in progress dies in the trenches. <b>Totalitarian ideologies</b> promise salvation: communism (a classless society – price: terror, the Gulag, famines), fascism/National Socialism (people, Führer, race – price: war and genocide). Democracies look weak and indecisive (appeasement). Culturally: the “Golden Twenties” (jazz, Bauhaus, cinema), existential philosophy (<b>Martin Heidegger</b>, later Sartre), quantum physics dismantles certainties. After 1945: “never again” – the United Nations, the Universal Declaration of Human Rights (1948); the Nuremberg trials found international criminal law.",
