    text: "<p>Europe is divided (the “Iron Curtain”), Germany doubly so – the Wall (1961) becomes the symbol. Crises on the edge of the abyss: the Berlin blockade (1948), the Cuban missile crisis (1962 – the world grazes past nuclear war), the Korean and Vietnam wars, Soviet invasions of Hungary (1956), Czechoslovakia (1968) and Afghanistan (1979).</p><p>In parallel, perhaps the greatest upheaval: <b>decolonization</b>. India becomes free in 1947 (Gandhi's non-violent resistance!); by ~1975 more than 80 new states emerge from the colonial empires – often with bloody birth pangs (the partition of India, the Algerian War, the Congo). Western Europe, meanwhile, lives its “economic miracle” and begins integration (EEC 1957 → EU). China turns communist in 1949; Mao's experiments (“Great Leap”, Cultural Revolution) cost tens of millions of lives before Deng Xiaoping dares market reform from 1978. 1969: the Moon landing – the high point of the contest of systems.</p>",
    denken: "Fear and awakening: <b>existentialism</b> (<b>Jean-Paul Sartre</b>, <b>Albert Camus</b> – man is condemned to be free), the anti-nuclear and peace movements, and from the 60s the great cultural revolt: the <b>civil rights movement</b> (Martin Luther King: “I have a dream”, 1963), the student protests of 1968, second-wave <b>feminism</b>, the beginnings of the <b>environmental movement</b> (1972, “The Limits to Growth”). Consumer society, television and pop culture (<b>The Beatles</b>!) change everyday life more radically than any ideology. In the end no thinker wins, but the attractiveness of prosperity and freedom – and Gorbachev's insight that the Soviet system cannot be reformed (glasnost, perestroika).",
